								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=812.msg13728#msg13728"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=812.msg13728#msg13728">Re: people don't need philanthropists, they need power!</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #9 on:</b> January 17, 2007, 05:53:55 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">I think you&#039;re gonna need to cite this. my uncle told me that macguiver saved america from the bloody commies.</div><br />the funny thing is that you hit the nail right on the head. my uncle would definitely spout out some shit like that. he&#039;s the most gullible guy ever, so he absorbs these ridiculous theories from tavern talk.<br /><br />i&#039;d read a similar article before and i share your concern. i just feel that the good deeds far outweigh the bad.<br /><br />&quot;•&nbsp; Chocolate companies said by the U.S. government to be profiting from the slave labor of children.&quot;<br /><br />this is the thing that concerns me the most, by far. the tobacco, alcohol, gaming, and lending industries don&#039;t really strike me as unworthy investments, so long as the returns benefit the world. that slave labor, though, really pisses me off and i&#039;m going to look into that a lot more (as a gates foundation donor, i&#039;m not too excited about my money supporting slave labor like it does when i buy clothes and so many other products).<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">But regardless, if foundations, and investors in general, invested primarily in socially responsible businesses/companies/corporations/organizations etc, not only would it shift the economy into a more socially responsible one (though still capitalistic), it would also decrease the need for such philanthropy to begin with. Socially/environmentally companies likely will not turn as much a profit as the Exxons and Walmats out there, so the foundations would in turn have less philanthropic capital to give, but I believe that strengthening companies that treat their workers well and promote a sustainable environment should be the primary concern.<br /><br />Last year when I was working for a local non-profit, my supervisor told a group of us in a meeting that without a proper perspective on the role of non-profits in the economy, you will basically become a parasite making your wealth because people are poor withouth really changing the reasons why people are poor. The same goes for any philanthropic or charitable cause, including the huge foundations.<br /><br />I&#039;d rather these foundations didn&#039;t exist at all rather than investing in companies that undermine the very causes they claim to support.</div><br />i&#039;m in your camp here, fasho (with the exception of the implication that capitalism is a regrettable phenomenon, which i disagree wholeheartedly with).</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td valign="bottom" class="smalltext" width="85%">
